<div class="intro">
<h2 style="margin-top:0">What is this report?</h2>
<p class="lede" style="font-size:1.02rem"><strong>{{ f.question }}</strong></p>
<p class="lede">Ulcerative colitis (UC) is a chronic inflammation of the lining of the colon. It starts in the rectum and spreads upwards, comes in flares, and is treated with drugs that calm the immune system: anti-TNF antibodies, antibodies against the gut-homing integrin and the IL-23 pathway, JAK inhibitors and S1P modulators. About half of patients do not respond well to any single drug, so researchers keep looking for new things to aim at (<em>targets</em>) and for measurements that tell patients apart (<em>biomarkers</em>).</p>
<p class="lede">This report is a map of that search. We took public data from thousands of colon biopsies and combined it with single cells, tissue sections, human genetics, the published literature and drug databases. For each of about {{ "{:,}".format(kpis.universe) }} human genes we ask the same question in many different ways: is it different in inflamed colon, which cell makes it, is the change the tissue itself or cells flooding in, does genetics say it is a cause, would a drug reach it, does it tell responders apart, and do other cohorts agree? A gene that looks good in one test and bad in the rest is not interesting. A gene that looks good in several unrelated tests is.</p>
<h3>How it was built, in four steps</h3>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Measure.</strong> Computer code runs every statistical test. It compares UC with control colon in eight cohorts, looks at single cells and tissue sections, tests genetic causality with Mendelian randomisation, and checks treatment trials. No language model touches the numbers.</li>
  <li><strong>Store with receipts.</strong> Each result goes into a knowledge graph as a small note that records the number, the dataset and the date. Nothing enters the graph without that note.</li>
  <li><strong>Ask the agents.</strong> Six AI agents, each with its own tools, read the graph and write short findings: expression, cells and tissue, pathways, literature, drugs and genetics, and how a candidate would hold up in the clinic. An orchestrator then writes the summary.</li>
  <li><strong>Check every sentence.</strong> Another piece of code checks each sentence the agents wrote. The note it cites must exist, it must be about the same gene, the numbers must match the note, and the note must not have changed since the agent read it. Sentences that fail are dropped. In this version {{ verification.statements if verification else "—" }} sentences were checked and {{ verification.unsupported if verification else "—" }} were unsupported.</li>
</ol>
<div class="note"><strong>How much to trust it.</strong> This is a prioritised list of hypotheses built from public data. It has not been tested in a laboratory or in patients, and it is not medical advice. Where the evidence is thin, the text says so. Where a result did not replicate, it is shown anyway.</div>
</div>
